(a) to abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the
National Flag and the National Anthem;
(b) to cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle
for freedom;
(c) to uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India;
(d) to defend the country and render national service when called upon to
do so;
(e) to promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all
the people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional or
sectional diversities; to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of
women;
(f) to value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture;
(g) to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes,
rivers, wildlife and to have compassion for living creatures;
(h) to develop the scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and
reform;
(i) to safeguard public property and to abjure violence;
(j) to strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective
activity so that the nation constantly rises to higher levels of endeavour
and achievement;
*(k) who is a parent or guardian, to provide opportunities for education to
his child or, as the case may be, ward between the age of six and
fourteen years.

$\bullet$ before law and equal protection of laws;
$\bullet$ irrespective of religion, race, caste, sex or place of birth;
$\bullet$ of opportunity in public employment;
$\bullet$ by abolition of untouchability and titles.

$\bullet$ of expression, assembly, association, movement, residence and profession;
$\bullet$ of certain protections in respect of conviction for offences;
$\bullet$ of protection of life and personal liberty;
$\bullet$ of free and compulsory education for children between the age of six and fourteen years;
$\bullet$ of protection against arrest and detention in certain cases.

$\bullet$ freedom of conscience and free profession, practice and propagation of religion;
$\bullet$ freedom to manage religious affairs;
$\bullet$ freedom as to payment of taxes for promotion of any particular religion;
$\bullet$ freedom as to attendance at religious instruction or religious worship in
educational institutions wholly maintained by the State.
